# atc A/B slot layout for a disk-booted qemu guest.
#
# Four partitions, GPT. The ESP carries GRUB, our grub.cfg, and the boot
# environment. The two rootfs slots are identical at build time: wic writes
# the same rootfs into both, so a fresh image boots slot A and holds a
# known-good slot B. The data partition holds the store.
#
# WHY THE STORE NEEDS ITS OWN PARTITION. atcd and its store live inside the
# rootfs. An OS update replaces a whole slot, so a store inside that slot is
# replaced with it. The device would lose its device id, its CAS, and its
# delivery history on every OS update. /var/atc therefore mounts from a
# partition no slot owns. The OS tier depends on it.
#
# THE LABELS AND THE ORDER ARE THE CONTRACT. grub.cfg finds the ESP by label,
# finds a slot by its partition NUMBER on the ESP's disk (gpt2 for A, gpt3 for
# B), and passes the slot's label as root=. RAUC's system.conf names the two
# slot devices by PARTLABEL. scripts/check-atc-ab-layout.sh reads all
# three files and fails when they disagree, the order of the parts included.
#
# ONE `--label` FEEDS TWO DIFFERENT NAMESPACES. wic passes the value to mkfs as
# the FILESYSTEM label, and on a GPT table it also writes it as the PARTITION
# name. The kernel's `root=PARTLABEL=` and RAUC's by-partlabel devices match the
# partition name, which no update changes. The filesystem label of a slot is
# gone after the first install: RAUC writes the bundle's ext4 image, which names
# no slot. That is why grub.cfg does not search for a slot by label.
bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=1

# The ESP. bootimg-efi generates its own EFI/BOOT/grub.cfg here, and
# atc-image-ab.bb overwrites it through IMAGE_EFI_BOOT_FILES, which the plugin
# copies AFTER the generated config is written. The `configfile` sourceparam
# would be the other route. It is not used: its path resolution searches the
# scripts directory and the layer by a rule this layer would have to match, and
# two mechanisms writing one file is a race nobody can read off the wks.
# NO MOUNTPOINT ON THIS PART, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# wic writes an /etc/fstab line for every part that HAS a mountpoint, and the
# device it writes is `/dev/<disk><n>` with the disk defaulting to `sda` unless
# the part carries --use-uuid or --use-label (poky
# scripts/lib/wic/plugins/imager/direct.py, update_fstab). This image boots off
# virtio, so that line names /dev/sda1 on a machine whose disk is /dev/vda.
#
# Measured on the first boot with a working serial console: systemd waited on a
# start job for /dev/sda1 for 90 seconds. The guest was otherwise healthy, and
# /var/atc had mounted correctly, because the image writes its own PARTLABEL
# line for that one and it won.
#
# Dropping the mountpoint makes wic skip the part entirely, so
# atc-ab-runtime.inc owns every fstab line and every one of them is a
# PARTLABEL. That matches `root=PARTLABEL=` on the kernel command line and the
# labels grub.cfg searches, so the whole image addresses partitions one way.
part --source bootimg-efi --sourceparams="loader=grub-efi" --label atcesp --active --align 1024 --fixed-size 64M

# Slot A. `--source rootfs` appears twice, which writes the SAME rootfs into
# both slots.
part / --source rootfs --fstype=ext4 --label atcroota --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024M

# Slot B, seeded identical so the first update has a known-good slot to revert
# to. `floor_flip_has_recoverable_prior` refuses to flip into a floor with no
# prior delivery for the same reason. A crash loop with nothing to fall back on
# is a stable brick.
part --source rootfs --fstype=ext4 --label atcrootb --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024M

# The store. Mounted at /var/atc by the fstab entry atc-ab-runtime.inc adds,
# and for the reason above this part carries no mountpoint either.
#
# 4096 MiB, not 512. An OS update lands in the store as a bundle, and the
# store's disk budget asks for the bundle plus a 1 GiB margin. On 512 MiB every
# OS push failed at every size. The .wic file stays sparse.
#
# The rootfs must NOT pre-create the directory: `atcd install-self` creates the
# tree with the modes the traverse-grant walk expects, and systemd creates a
# missing mount point on its own.
part --fstype=ext4 --label atcdata --align 1024 --fixed-size 4096M
